/// Why a retained unit enablement row is DORMANT — present, not serving, and
/// not the unit's own doing (#369, #370, D-CK on #59).
///
/// Dormancy is the answer to "what is a row that cannot legitimately serve?".
/// The row is kept rather than retired, for the same reason uninstall retains
/// unit rows behind a tombstone (D-AS, D-BJ): a re-scope back or a conforming
/// config restores the household's settings instead of destroying them. The
/// serving predicate excludes a dormant row, so nothing consumes it meanwhile.
///
/// SINGLE-valued with a precedence, per D-CP: one activation can both narrow
/// scope and tighten `config.schema`, and ScopeOrphaned outranks
/// NeedsConfiguration because a row whose plugin scope no longer admits
/// household enablement cannot be healed by supplying config — naming the
/// config reason there would be unactionable advice.
enum PluginUnitDormantReason {
  /// The plugin's scope no longer admits household enablement: an activation
  /// re-scoped it household→server, and household-scope consent has no
  /// collection point there (#369). Healed only by a re-scope back.
  ScopeOrphaned

  /// The retained `config` document no longer satisfies the active
  /// `config.schema` (#370). Set unconditionally on that mismatch — NOT
  /// gated on the manifest's `requiresHouseholdConfig`, which decides only
  /// whether a document is mandatory, not whether the schema binds the one
  /// a row already holds. Healed by an admin supplying a conforming
  /// document — which is exactly what the enable/config-PATCH gate
  /// validates.
  NeedsConfiguration
}
